<h2>Country comparison</h2>
<ul>
<li><strong>United States</strong> — premium demand, tariff exposure and local-content incentives; domestic cell capacity is growing but not yet sufficient for all segments.</li>
<li><strong>Latin America</strong> — import-led replacement and two-wheeler demand, with solar-storage projects in Chile, Brazil and Mexico.</li>
<li><strong>Canada</strong> — resource and automotive supply chain, closely linked to US rules of origin.</li>
</ul>
<h2>What to verify</h2>
<p>Confirm the tariff classification, the country of origin and the applicable trade programme before quoting. A product that qualifies under one origin rule may not qualify under another; the difference can be larger than the factory price gap between suppliers.</p>
<p class="muted">Related: <a href="/regions/asia/">Asia</a> · <a href="/regions/europe/">Europe</a> · <a href="/prices/">Prices</a></p>
<h2>A note on lead times</h2>
<p>US buyers usually face shorter ocean transit but more port and rail variability; Latin American buyers face longer transit and more customs variability. Ask for a landed lead time with the route and the Incoterm, not an ex-works production date. The difference between the two is often two to four weeks.</p>
<h2>Frequently asked questions</h2>
<h3>Do US tariffs apply to all battery imports?</h3>
<p>No. The rate depends on the product, the country of origin and the trade programme in force. Check the current tariff schedule and the origin rules for your specific HS code.</p>
<h3>Is Latin America a single market?</h3>
<p>No. Brazil, Mexico, Chile and the Andean markets differ in tariffs, certification and demand mix. Treat the region as a set of country markets, not one.</p>
